<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  HartClass: 7 entry categorical colormap for HCPSclass (the joint Hart
  CPS class, see D2D/README.md and cps_HartCPS.hart_class). Pair this with a
  styleRule range of -0.5 to 6.5 so each integer code below lands in the
  middle of one color bin instead of on a boundary between two:

    code 0  symmetric deep warm core                    red      (0.85, 0.15, 0.15, 1.0)
    code 1  symmetric shallow warm core                 magenta  (0.80, 0.20, 0.75, 1.0)
    code 2  asymmetric deep warm core                   yellow   (0.98, 0.85, 0.10, 1.0)
    code 3  asymmetric shallow warm core                green    (0.20, 0.68, 0.25, 1.0)
    code 4  asymmetric cold core                        blue     (0.15, 0.50, 0.90, 1.0)
    code 5  symmetric cold core                         indigo   (0.35, 0.22, 0.72, 1.0)
    code 6  shallow cold core (lower cold, upper warm)  gray     (0.72, 0.72, 0.70, 1.0)

  The hues form three groups so the Evans and Hart (2003) onset and
  completion read as abrupt color shifts: reds for a symmetric warm core
  (0 red, 1 magenta), yellow and green for a storm in transition (2
  yellow after onset, 3 green after the upper warm core is lost), blues
  for a cold core (4 blue after completion, 5 indigo once symmetric).
  Code 6 is neutral gray.

  File format: a root <colorMap> element containing an ordered list of
  <color r="" g="" b="" a=""/> entries, each component a float from
  0.0 to 1.0, in code order (this file's first <color> is code 0, and
  so on). VERIFY the element/attribute names against your site's actual
  .cmap schema.
-->
<colorMap>
        <color r="0.85" g="0.15" b="0.15" a="1.0"/>
        <color r="0.80" g="0.20" b="0.75" a="1.0"/>
        <color r="0.98" g="0.85" b="0.10" a="1.0"/>
        <color r="0.20" g="0.68" b="0.25" a="1.0"/>
        <color r="0.15" g="0.50" b="0.90" a="1.0"/>
        <color r="0.35" g="0.22" b="0.72" a="1.0"/>
        <color r="0.72" g="0.72" b="0.70" a="1.0"/>
</colorMap>
